<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#agent-orange">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agent Orange</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1962">1962</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agent Orange was a herbicide sprayed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> military over forests and crops in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, mainly between 1962 and 1971. It was contaminated with the toxic dioxin TCDD, which has been linked to cancers, birth defects and other illnesses among Vietnamese civilians and veterans. Clean-up of contaminated former air bases continued into the 2020s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agent-orange.jpg" alt="Agent Orange" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: US Army; derivative work by Georgfotoart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20130526234147!US-Huey-helicopter-spraying-Agent-Orange-in-Vietnam_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Agent Orange was one of a group of tactical-use herbicides known as the Rainbow Herbicides. Its active ingredient was an equal mixture of two phenoxy herbicides, 2,4-D and 2,4,5-T, and it contained traces of the dioxin TCDD, typically 2 to 3 parts per million. Several herbicides were developed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom as weapons during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Agent Orange itself was produced in the United States from the late 1940s for industrial agriculture and for clearing undergrowth along railways and power lines. The British had experimented with defoliants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a>, which influenced its later use by the United States.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In mid-1961 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-dinh-diem">Ngo Dinh Diem</a> of South Vietnam asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to help defoliate the jungle that gave cover to the North Vietnamese army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a>. In August the South Vietnamese air force conducted herbicide operations with American help. Many American officials supported the idea, noting that the British had used herbicides in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, and in November 1961 President Kennedy authorised Operation Ranch Hand, the code name for the United States Air Force's herbicide programme, which ran from 1962 to 1971 and was formally directed by the government of South Vietnam. The military procured over 20,000,000 United States gallons, and nine chemical companies produced it, among them Dow Chemical and Monsanto.</p><p>The spraying also aimed to destroy crops. A RAND Corporation memorandum argued that because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a> obtained most of their food from the rural population, an effective programme would have to destroy large parts of the rural economy. Crops were sprayed and areas bulldozed, which forced many rural civilians to move to cities. About 17.8 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s forested area, some 31,000 square kilometres, was sprayed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> states that 4 million of its citizens were exposed and that as many as 3 million have been ill because of it. The Vietnamese Red Cross estimates that up to 1 million people are disabled or have health problems. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government has called these figures unreliable. Exposure has been linked to birth defects, cancers, cardiovascular disease and diabetes, though the science on causation remains incomplete. Some lawyers, historians and academics, and the Swedish prime minister Olof Palme, have described the destruction of forest as ecocide.</p><p>In 1978 the veteran Paul Reutershan sued Dow Chemical, and his case became a class action for thousands of veterans. The Agent Orange Act of 1991 let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Veterans Affairs declare certain conditions presumptive of exposure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agent Orange” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new state joined the United Nations in 1977 and inherited a country of some 49 million people devastated by three decades of war, with damaged infrastructure, unexploded ordnance and land poisoned by herbicides such as <b>Agent Orange</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
